---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';

export const prerender = true;

const title = `About — ${SITE_TITLE}`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={title} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
		<style>
			.page-shell {
				width: 760px;
				max-width: calc(100% - 2em);
				margin: auto;
				padding: 1em 1em 4em;
			}
			.intro {
				margin: 2em 0 2.5em;
			}
			.eyebrow {
				margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
				color: rgb(var(--gray));
				font-size: 0.8rem;
				letter-spacing: 0.12em;
				text-transform: uppercase;
			}
			.intro h1 {
				margin: 0 0 0.5em;
				line-height: 1.1;
			}
			.lede,
			section p,
			.outro {
				line-height: 1.7;
				color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
			}
			.lede {
				margin: 0;
				font-size: 1.1rem;
			}
			section {
				margin-bottom: 2.25em;
			}
			section h2 {
				margin: 0 0 0.6em;
				font-size: 1.05rem;
			}
			section p {
				margin: 0 0 1em;
			}
			section p:last-child {
				margin-bottom: 0;
			}
			.outro {
				margin: 0;
				font-size: 0.95rem;
			}
			a {
				color: rgb(var(--accent));
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main class="page-shell">
			<header class="intro">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1>An NBA tracker that keeps its own numbers.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					{SITE_TITLE} has two halves: a tracker for the NBA slate, and a catalogue of drills for
					the training sessions behind it. The scores on these pages are read from the site's own
					cache, not fetched from the provider while you wait.
				</p>
			</header>

			<section>
				<h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
				<p>
					The games come from balldontlie, fetched by the server rather than by the browser. A slate
					is refreshed at most once a minute while a game is in progress, and not at all for half a
					day once it has settled, so a page you open twice costs one request instead of two. Every
					call is counted against a shared daily budget: a busy day degrades into older numbers
					rather than a dead key.
				</p>
				<p>
					Each page prints the age of the snapshot it rendered. When a refresh is refused, the page
					shows the last numbers it has and says the feed could not be reached, because a stale score
					that announces itself is more useful than an error page.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What a game page shows</h2>
				<p>
					The line score, quarter by quarter, with an overtime column when the game needed one.
					Tip-off, the stage — regular season, playoffs or the NBA Cup round — and both teams with
					the division and conference on file. While a game is being played it also shows timeouts
					remaining and which team is in the bonus, and those two rows disappear once the game is
					final: upstream leaves a finished game's last values in place, and repeating them would
					describe play that is over.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What is deliberately missing</h2>
				<p>
					There is no box score yet. The provider plan behind this deployment does not include the
					statistics endpoint, so the game page says the box score is not available rather than
					filling a table with numbers nobody measured. The scores and quarter lines above it are
					real and come from the cache.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>The drills</h2>
				<p>
					The catalogue beside the tracker is content, not a table: one markdown file per drill,
					each naming the measures a logged session records — makes and attempts, sets, reps, or
					weight — so a drill cannot promise a number the database has nowhere to put. Adding one
					is a pull request that adds a file.
				</p>
			</section>

			<p class="outro">
				The site is an Astro app rendered on the server and deployed to ECS by CDK, and every change
				reaches it through a pull request and a pipeline. <a href="/nba">Start with today's slate</a>,
				or <a href="/drills">browse the drills</a>.
			</p>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
